  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the mid/upper-levels, a neutrally-tilting, shortwave trough was \npivoting across the northern Great Lakes, with slightly negative \ngeopotential height tendencies over the region that were objectively \nanalyzed by the 05/00Z UA charts. Farther south, the core of a weak, \nsubtropical ridge was centered near the FL Straits, which will allow \nfor a quick progression of the aforementioned shortwave trough as it \nbecomes negatively-tilted and vertically-stacked while entering the \nNorth Atlantic over the next 24+ hours. The northwestern periphery \nof a thick cirrus shield continues to advect northeastward over the \neastern fringes of the CWA, as the split in the high-level flow \nacross the Lower 48 is phasing over the Eastern Seaboard following \nthe ejection of the shortwave trough, with a clear sky along and \nwest of the I-79 and HWY-119 corridors. \n\nAt the surface, a weak low was rotating eastward across northern \nLake Erie, with the synoptic cold front drawn southwestward along a \nline from ERI-CMH-CVG. The prefrontal airmass has undergone rapid \nmoistening even beneath the northwestern edge of the cirrostratus \nshield, with widespread fog evident across the mountains into the \nlowlands. Fog has developed beyond the normal river valleys, and a \nSpecial Weather Statement has been issued for all WV counties and \nthe far western VA counties until 13Z, or 9 AM EDT. Visibilities may \nfall to one-quarter mile or less for some locales. As the cold front \ncontinues to move towards the southeast, dry advection from the cP \nairmass trailing behind will cause fog to mix out even before \nsunrise for the northwestern zones. Light, northwesterly winds will \naccompany the cold front, with fog mixing out entirely near or \nshortly after 13Z. Highs today will be a few degrees cooler than \nyesterday, and dewpoints were also lowered from the NBM due to the \ndry, cP airmass settling into the region. Post-frontal winds will \ndiminish area-wide after dark.\n\nOvernight, chilly-to-cold temperatures are expected to evolve as the \ncombination of a clear sky and calm winds maximize the effects of \nradiational cooling. Frost is expected across the northeastern \nmountains of WV tonight, and a Frost Advisory has been issued for \nBarbour, Pocahontas, and Randolph Counties between 06/04-13Z. Low \ntemperatures for those locales will fall to near 33-34 degrees, with \nlows in the middle-upper 30s elsewhere across the mountainous \nregions of the CWA while ranging from the upper 30s to middle 40s \nacross the lowlands. Persistence forecasting was applied to Tuesday, \nas little change in the airmass is expected compared to Monday. Low \ntemperatures for Wednesday morning may need to be adjusted a few \ndegrees lower than what is currently forecast, with frost possible \nonce again for the northeastern mountains of WV. However, with winds \ntransitioning towards the south as return flow becomes established \nahead of another shortwave trough emerging over the Great Lakes \nregion, there remains some uncertainty on the extent of frost \nWednesday morning. A gradual warm-up will follow on Wednesday as \nsurface winds veer southwestward, with persistent NVA aloft keeping \nthe region dry even as another cold front passes through the region \nby Thursday due to the glancing influence of the broad troughing to \nthe north of the CWA. PoPs are NIL through Saturday.",
